{"city":{"id":"higashiyama","name":"Higashiyama","country":"Japan","country_code":"JP","timezone":"Asia/Tokyo","locale":"en-JP","description":"Kyoto's Higashiyama: Kiyomizu-dera's wooden stage over the valley, the stone lanes of Sannenzaka and Ninenzaka with their noren and lanterns, and the Yasaka Pagoda over the roofs.","vertical_datum":"m T.P. (Tokyo Peil)","year":2026,"tour":[{"title":"Old Kyoto on the hillside","text":"The lanes climb from Yasaka-dori to the gates of Kiyomizu-dera, past teahouses, pottery shops and pickle sellers. Come early, before the tour buses, or at dusk when the lanterns come on.","cam":[-520,-640,420],"tgt":[120,-120,90],"date":[11,20],"from":"15:00","to":"16:00","hold":8},{"title":"Kiyomizu-dera","text":"The Main Hall's stage hangs nearly 13 m over the valley on a lattice of keyaki pillars, joined without a single nail. \"To jump from the stage of Kiyomizu\" is how Kyoto says taking the plunge.","cam":[350,-300,126],"tgt":[268,-248,114],"date":[11,20],"from":"15:30","to":"16:30","hold":8},{"title":"Sannenzaka and Ninenzaka","text":"Stone steps between two-storey wooden shops, noren in the doorways, lanterns at the eaves. Legend says a stumble on Sannenzaka brings three years' bad luck, so watch your step.","cam":[-150,-40,110],"tgt":[-108,60,84],"date":[11,20],"from":"16:20","to":"17:00","hold":8},{"title":"The Yasaka Pagoda","text":"Five storeys of bare timber, 46 m high, rebuilt in 1440: the picture of Kyoto, at the top of the lane, best framed from Yasaka-dori at sunset.","cam":[-340,182,68],"tgt":[-250,173,84],"date":[11,20],"from":"16:30","to":"17:10","hold":8}],"start":{"date":[11,20],"time":"15:40","cam":[-520,-640,420],"tgt":[120,-120,90]},"protection":{"title":"No statutory sun rule for public places","summary":"Kyoto protects its skyline with height limits (a cap of 31 m in the busiest districts) and Japan limits shadows on homes with the Building Standards Act, but neither protects sun on parks. Sun on these places is measured for information, not tested against a rule.","source_note":"No legal window is transcribed for these places, so each is shown for 10:00-14:00 and none is marked protected.","reading_note":"Heights come from Project PLATEAU, measured from aerial survey in 2020-2021, so buildings finished since are missing or low.","default_time_basis":"clock","tip":"Tip: the hillside puts the lanes in shade early on winter afternoons.","unprotected_label":"no rule"},"minor_labels":[],"place_labels":[],"sun_hours_region":[-880,880,-860,860],"lat":34.997,"lon":135.782,"architecture":"japan","signage":0.3},"frame":{"proj":"+proj=tmerc +lat_0=34.997 +lon_0=135.782 +k=1 +x_0=0 +y_0=0 +ellps=WGS84 +units=m +no_defs","lon0":135.782,"lat0":34.997,"x":[-900,900],"y":[-880,880],"units":"m, z = m T.P. (Tokyo Peil)"},"terrain":{"file":"terrain.json","nx":181,"ny":177,"cell":10.0,"scale":0.1,"origin":"north-west, row-major, rows go south","bare_earth":true},"ground":[{"file":"ground_0_0.jpg","x0":-900.0,"x1":100.0,"y0":-320.0,"y1":880.0},{"file":"ground_1_0.jpg","x0":100.0,"x1":1100.0,"y0":-320.0,"y1":880.0},{"file":"ground_0_1.jpg","x0":-900.0,"x1":100.0,"y0":-1520.0,"y1":-320.0},{"file":"ground_1_1.jpg","x0":100.0,"x1":1100.0,"y0":-1520.0,"y1":-320.0}],"shore":{"file":"shore.png","cell":2.5,"nx":720,"ny":704,"x0":-900,"y1":880,"encoding":"r: 128 + 2 x signed distance to the shoreline (m, water positive); g: sand; b: rock","slopes":{"sand":0.04,"rock":0.25,"built":1.0},"floor_m":4.0,"sand_polygons":0,"rock_polygons":0},"water":{},"trees":{"mix":{"zelkova":0.2,"ginkgo":0.14,"camphor":0.12,"cherry":0.14,"maple":0.16,"pine":0.1,"evergreen":0.14},"woods":{"evergreen":0.38,"sugi":0.22,"camphor":0.1,"maple":0.06,"pine":0.1,"zelkova":0.08,"cherry":0.06},"note":"Species are not mapped; the mix is a guess from Kyoto's street-tree surveys and the temples' own guides. The streets have zelkovas (keyaki), ginkgos, camphors and cherries; the Higashiyama hillsides are a laurel forest of evergreen oaks and chinquapins (evergreen) with cedars (sugi, standing for hinoki too), black pines and maples. Areas give the precincts known for their own trees a mix of their own: Kiyomizu-dera's thousand maples and fifteen hundred cherries, Maruyama Park's cherries round the Gion weeping cherry, Kodai-ji's maples. Their outlines are OpenStreetMap's (pipeline/tree_areas.py).","areas":[{"name":"Kiyomizu-dera","find":"\u6e05\u6c34\u5bfa","mix":{"maple":0.42,"cherry":0.3,"pine":0.1,"evergreen":0.18},"woods":{"maple":0.34,"cherry":0.16,"evergreen":0.3,"sugi":0.12,"pine":0.08},"note":"About a thousand maples and fifteen hundred cherries round the Stage and down the Nishikigumo valley.","xy":[[63,-252],[77,-324],[100,-374],[228,-495],[329,-486],[358,-345],[390,-265],[335,-131],[310,-99],[237,-77],[131,-85],[115,-112],[86,-110],[88,-168],[71,-168],[76,-219],[66,-231]],"osm":"W336641107"},{"name":"Gion weeping cherry","at":[-100.8,763.5],"radius":3,"mix":{"shidarezakura":1},"yozakura":true,"note":"Maruyama Park's Gion shidarezakura (the second, grown from the first's seed), about 12 m tall, planted on its own spot as a landmark tree (city.py gion_cherry, landmarks.json trees): the lone round crown in the plaza in the aerial photograph."},{"name":"Maruyama Park","find":"\u5186\u5c71\u516c\u5712","mix":{"cherry":0.48,"shidarezakura":0.12,"maple":0.15,"pine":0.1,"zelkova":0.15},"yozakura":true,"note":"Kyoto's oldest public park, famous for its cherries round the Gion weeping cherry: about 680, Somei-yoshino and weeping cherries (the park's guide).","xy":[[-379,799],[-368,799],[-362,786],[-275,791],[-263,778],[-257,702],[-141,711],[-132,700],[-125,661],[-9,652],[40,637],[49,669],[151,650],[167,766],[159,798],[125,814],[106,817],[59,764],[31,756],[-8,767],[-101,825],[-116,796],[-127,794],[-252,850],[-325,862],[-343,850]],"osm":"W54170783"},{"name":"Kodai-ji","find":"\u9ad8\u53f0\u5bfa","mix":{"maple":0.45,"pine":0.15,"evergreen":0.2,"cherry":0.12,"shidarezakura":0.08},"note":"Kodai-ji's weeping c